### 1. Vulnerable code location

`BlogController.Index()` only gates `password`-status blogs; `private` and `draft` blogs are rendered without any ownership or visibility check:

```go
func (c *BlogController) Index() {
    c.Prepare()
    c.TplName = "blog/index.tpl"
    blogId, _ := strconv.Atoi(c.Ctx.Input.Param(":id"))
    ...
    blog, err := models.NewBlog().FindFromCache(blogId)
    ...
    if c.Ctx.Input.IsPost() {
        password := c.GetString("password")
        if blog.BlogStatus == "password" && password != blog.Password {
            c.JsonResult(6001, ...)
        } else if blog.BlogStatus == "password" && password == blog.Password {
            _ = c.CruSession.Set(context.TODO(), blogReadSession, blogId)
            c.JsonResult(0, "OK")
        } else {
            c.JsonResult(0, "OK")
        }
    } else if blog.BlogStatus == "password" && c.CruSession.Get(...) == nil &&
        (c.Member == nil || (blog.MemberId != c.Member.MemberId && !c.Member.IsAdministrator())) {
        c.TplName = "blog/index_password.tpl"   // Gate exists ONLY for password-status blogs
    }
    // private / draft blogs: NO ownership check, NO visibility check — rendered directly
    c.Data["Model"] = blog
    c.Data["Content"] = template.HTML(blog.BlogRelease)
    ...
}
```

Private-status blog posts do not appear in any list page (home page, personal blog list, or public browse), making this a "hidden direct-URL read" — invisible in listings but fully readable by direct access.

### 2. PoC

Detection procedure: the administrator account (highvictim) created a blog post with `status=private` (blog_id=1), injecting the canary `private_information` into the **title** and `private_information` into the **summary**. The post does not appear on the home page or any blog listing (verified). The attacker account (attacker, regular user) then directly accessed the blog using **its own session cookie**:

```http
GET /blog-1.html HTTP/1.1
Host: 192.168.233.134:9011
Cookie: csrftoken=<attacker-csrftoken>; sessionid=<attacker-sessionid>
```

Response (HTTP 200, `<title>private_information - ...`) contains the private blog content that the attacker has no permission to read:

- `<title>` renders the private blog **title** canary `private_information`
- The page body renders the **summary/content** canary `private_information`

Both canaries are present in the attacker's response, proving full disclosure of the private blog's title and content.

### 3. Impact

- Any authenticated user can enumerate the blog ID (sequential) to read all `private` and `draft` blog posts of every user, including administrators;
- Private blog posts are hidden from all list pages, so victims cannot detect unauthorized reads;
